<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- REQUIRED UNDER THE HARDENED RUNTIME, which `build-app.sh` turns on for any Developer ID
       signature because notarisation refuses a submission without it. The hardened runtime blocks
       Apple Events by default: without this key every `osascript` this app runs — reveal a file in
       Finder, open a folder in Terminal or iTerm, read the terminal session list — fails with
       errAEEventNotPermitted (-1743), and it fails ONLY in the notarised build, never in the local
       one. That is the shape of a bug found by a customer.

       `NSAppleEventsUsageDescription` in Info.plist is a DIFFERENT thing and both are needed: the
       Info.plist string is the consent prompt the user sees, this entitlement is the runtime
       permission to ask at all.

       Nothing else is listed on purpose. Chute reads and writes the user's own files as the user,
       and the CLI it ships spawns git — so no library-validation exception, no JIT, no unsigned
       executable memory, no disable-executable-page-protection. Each of those is a question at
       App Review and buys nothing here. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key><true/>
</dict>
</plist>
